==English==

===Etymology===
* {{sense|adjective}} {{inh|en|ang|willende}}, present participle of {{m|ang|willan}}
* {{sense|noun}} {{inh|en|ang|willung}}, from {{m|ang|willian}}
{{surf|en|will|-ing}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈwɪlɪŋ/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-willing.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|will|ing}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪlɪŋ|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[ready|Ready]] to do something, particularly something that requires [[change]] or [[effort]]; not [[object#Verb|objecting]].
#: {{syn|en|agreeable|agreeing|consenting|voluntary|up to|Thesaurus:acquiescent}}
#: {{ux|en|If my boyfriend isn't '''willing''' to change his drinking habits, I will split up with him.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=In the eyes of Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke the apotheosis of the Celebrity was complete. The people of Asquith were not only '''willing''' to attend the house-warming, but had been worked up to the pitch of eagerness. The Celebrity as a matter of course was master of ceremonies.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-07|author=David Simpson
|volume=188|issue=26|page=36|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/books/2013/may/18/falling-upwards-richard-holmes-review Fantasy of navigation]
|passage=Like most human activities, ballooning has sponsored heroes and hucksters and a good deal in between. For every dedicated scientist patiently recording atmospheric pressure and wind speed while shivering at high altitudes, there is a carnival barker with a bevy of pretty girls '''willing''' to dangle from a basket or parachute down to earth.}}

